00:00:01.455 --> 00:00:03.080
JEN PERSON: Welcome
to "Getting Started

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.444
with Artifact Registry."

00:00:05.444 --> 00:00:08.480
Today, I'll show you how to
deploy a container stored

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.430
in an Artifact
Registry repository

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.980
to Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:14.660
If you're new the
Artifact Registry,

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.640
I recommend you check
out the last episode

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.680
of "Getting Started"
or the documentation

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.060
before proceeding.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.438
With that in mind, let's walk
through deploying to GKE.

00:00:23.438 --> 00:00:25.980
Now there's a good chance that
if you're watching this video,

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.190
you already know about
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:32.090
but to get everyone up to speed,
let's have a quick overview.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.550
GKE is an enterprise
grade platform

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.740
for containerized
applications, including

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:40.820
stateful and stateless, AI
and ML, Linux and Windows,

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:45.680
complex and simple web apps,
API and backend services.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.710
You can use GKE to leverage
industry-first features,

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:52.070
like four-way auto scaling
and no-stress management.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.760
You can also optimize
GPU and TPU provisioning,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.090
use integrated developer tools,
and get multi cluster support

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:00.650
from SREs.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:04.910
So why use Artifact
Registry then with GKE?

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:07.220
Using Artifact Registry
with GKE gives you

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.950
the ability to manage all of
your artifacts in one place.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.910
So for example, you can
build Maven packages,

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:16.197
push them to Artifact
Registry, containerize them

00:01:16.197 --> 00:01:20.080
with Cloud Build in Jib,
and then deploy them to GKE,

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.280
all while using
consistent IM across the

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.170
build process, artifact
management, and GKE.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.270
Artifact Registry also
enables you to run

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.660
in fully regionalized stacks.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.650
So with Artifact Registry,
an Australian or South

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:36.170
American customer
can run GKE clusters

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.820
and manage container
images in the same region,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.260
and pay no egress costs
while they pull images.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.460
Pretty sweet, huh?

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.480
So without further ado,
let's deploy to GKE.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.430
First, start with
new or existing code.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:52.680
Here's a simple
Go helloworld app

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.550
that I'm going to
use in my container.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.480
You can also take a
look at my Dockerfile

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.600
here for kicks, though it's
pretty much boilerplate.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.460
See below for a link
to the documentation

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.980
where you can see
this file in detail.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:07.250
I'm using Cloud Build to build
this project, but keep in mind

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.900
that you don't have to.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.330
Using Cloud Build allows
you to automatically push

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.210
the image to Artifact Registry,
which I like because it

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:17.030
streamlines the deploy process.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:20.240
These next couple steps are
for building with Cloud Build

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.920
if you don't already know
your cloud project ID,

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.820
you can get it by
running this command.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:29.600
Now create a file
called cloudbuild.yaml.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.160
Here's the contents
of that file,

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.130
but don't worry about rushing
to pause the video so you

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:35.587
can copy this down.

00:02:35.587 --> 00:02:37.670
You can find this code in
the documentation, which

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:40.940
of course is linked below.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.520
These variables,
repository and image,

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.710
are used for the repository
name and the name

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:47.810
of the image in
the repository so

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.330
that they can be replaced
at built time using custom

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.640
Cloud Build substitutions.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.550
This is very useful if you want
to use the same build config

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.040
file to push images
to repositories

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.950
for different environments,
such as testing, staging, and/or

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:02.150
production.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.760
For example, this
command would substitute

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.050
draynepipe for the
repository name,

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.430
and my image for the image name.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.260
I run this command to
submit my container

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.990
to build using Cloud Build.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:17.630
It is then stored
using Artifact Registry

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.650
in the draynepipe repository.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.190
Now that you have a project
built and ready to go,

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:25.250
you may need to configure
access to your container.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:27.770
Check the docs to see
the minimum required GKE

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:29.840
versions to create
clusters that have

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.700
default permissions
to pull containers

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.760
from Docker repositories
in the same project.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.390
If you are not using one
of these GKE versions,

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:38.840
you will need to
configure access

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.380
to pull containers using
Kubernetes imagePullSecrets.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.840
If you need to access
containers from repositories

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:46.490
in another project,
then you need

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:50.120
to grant read permission on
that repository to the identity

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.190
being used to pull the image.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.160
Once that access
is configured, you

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.920
can run an Artifact Registry
image on a GKE cluster

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.230
using this command.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:03.140
Where "name" is the name
you've given the object,

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.900
"location" is the regional
or multi-regional location

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.550
of your repository,
"project ID" is, of course,

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:12.290
your Google Cloud project ID.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:14.660
"Repository" is the
name of the repository

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.430
where the image is stored.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.890
"Image" is the name of the
image in the repository,

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:21.140
and "tag" is the tag
for the image version

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:22.700
that you want to pull.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:28.390
So for my draynepipe project,
I'd use this command.

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:31.520
And now my image that's
stored in an Artifact Registry

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.450
repository is deployed to GKE.

